th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028+v3-2026-11-02+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+type-mix-retire-20261112+type-mix-topup-20261112+type-mix-retire2-20261112+fill-20261112+fill2-20261112","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Programming-for-EOR/distribution-functions-rdpq.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Programming-for-EOR/distribution-functions-rdpq.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,0.5^2)$, the call `dnorm(0, mean = 0, sd = 0.5)` returns `[1] 0.7978846`. What is this number?","options":["The height of the density curve of $X$ above the point $x=0$.","The probability $\\mathbb{P}(X=0)$ that $X$ takes exactly the value 0.","The probability $\\mathbb{P}(X\\le0)$, the area under the density left of 0.","The probability $\\mathbb{P}(-0.5\\le X\\le0.5)$ within one standard deviation."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d69d2f3a136e6a521dc70303","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which probability does `pnorm(3, 2)` compute, given that the normal functions have the arguments `q`, `mean = 0` and `sd = 1` in that order?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(X\\le3)$ for $X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,2^2)$, as the 2 is taken as `sd`","None: R stops with an error, as the argument `sd` is missing","$\\mathbb{P}(X\\le3)$ for $X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(2,1)$, as `sd` keeps its default 1","$\\mathbb{P}(2\\le Z\\le3)$ for a standard normal $Z$, the area between 2 and 3"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d25b9bd87dcb615b78164a2e","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In the binomial family of R functions, for which function must the first argument be a probability between 0 and 1?","options":["`pbinom()`","`qbinom()`","`dbinom()`","`rbinom()`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0455609ea1c6dc7ae5fc009c","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"The number $X$ of customers arriving in a minute is Poisson with mean 2. Which call returns $\\mathbb{P}(X=3)$?","options":["`ppois(3, lambda = 2)`","`qpois(3, lambda = 2)`","`rpois(3, lambda = 2)`","`dpois(3, lambda = 2)`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f4ed066902bd2769f83e340b","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A count $X$ is binomial with 20 trials and success probability 0.1. Which call draws 100 independent values of $X$?","options":["`rbinom(20, size = 100, prob = 0.1)`","`rbinom(100, size = 20, prob = 0.1)`","`dbinom(100, size = 20, prob = 0.1)`","`sum(rbinom(20, size = 1, prob = 0.1))`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0191e2297a826a802c564e61","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"`pnorm(1.5)` returns `[1] 0.9331928`. What does `pnorm(-1.5)` return?","options":["`[1] 0.0668072`","`[1] -0.9331928`","`[1] 0.9331928`","`[1] 0.8663856`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0267bf8d985a0a53f91913c1","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does `qnorm(0.975)` return?","options":["`[1] 1.644854`","`[1] 0.8352199`","`[1] 1.959964`","`[1] -1.959964`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c9c5ebc9007dc7eb3a4c52df","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Exam scores $X$ are normal with mean 50 and variance 100. Which call returns the score $c$ with $\\mathbb{P}(X>c)=0.05$?","options":["`qnorm(0.05, mean = 50, sd = 10)`","`pnorm(0.95, mean = 50, sd = 10)`","`qnorm(0.95, mean = 50, sd = 100)`","`qnorm(0.95, mean = 50, sd = 10)`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9fac085ec31c88a215d98dec","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a binomial count with 4 trials and success probability 0.3, which expression returns the same value as `pbinom(2, size = 4, prob = 0.3)`?","options":["`sum(dbinom(1:2, size = 4, prob = 0.3))`","`sum(dbinom(0:2, size = 4, prob = 0.3))`","`dbinom(2, size = 4, prob = 0.3)`","`sum(dbinom(0:1, size = 4, prob = 0.3))`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f10030a3051af34e311beb28","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A waiting time $X$ is exponential with mean 5 minutes. Which call returns $\\mathbb{P}(X\\le2)$?","options":["`pexp(2, rate = 0.2)`","`pexp(2, rate = 5)`","`dexp(2, rate = 0.2)`","`1 - pexp(2, rate = 0.2)`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fcb48fbfe65182d9cf23e0d0","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A waiting time $X$ is exponential with rate 0.5, so its mean is $1/0.5=2$. Which statement about `qexp(0.5, rate = 0.5)` is correct?","options":["It returns the mean of $X$, 2, because the 0.5 level is the centre.","It returns $\\mathbb{P}(X\\le0.5)$, about 0.22, the area left of 0.5.","It returns the median of $X$, about 1.39, which lies below the mean 2.","It returns 0, the value $x$ where the density equals $f(x)=0.5$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d650caa3b319d7c305e66879","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a Poisson count $X$ with mean 3, which probability does `ppois(5, lambda = 3, lower.tail = FALSE)` return?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(X\\ge5)$","$\\mathbb{P}(X\\le5)$","$\\mathbb{P}(X<5)$","$\\mathbb{P}(X>5)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7088f536f0b00e32b4cbeb80","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The number $X$ of calls in an hour is Poisson with mean 4; the second argument of `ppois()` is `lambda`. Which expression returns $\\mathbb{P}(2\\le X\\le5)$?","options":["`ppois(5, 4) - ppois(1, 4)`","`ppois(5, 4) - ppois(2, 4)`","`dpois(5, 4) - dpois(2, 4)`","`ppois(4, 4) - ppois(1, 4)`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"772fec559516bea35e674a3a","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A waiting time $X$ is uniform on the interval from 0 to 0.5 minutes, and the uniform functions take the arguments `min` and `max`. What does `dunif(0.3, min = 0, max = 0.5)` return?","options":["`[1] 0.6`","`[1] 2`","`[1] 1`","`[1] 0.5`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"02da6ceced7e8e206a381508","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"`qnorm(0.9, mean = 10, sd = 4)` returns `[1] 15.12621`. What does `qnorm(0.1, mean = 10, sd = 4)` return?","options":["`[1] -15.12621`","`[1] -5.126206`","`[1] 4.873794`","`[1] 8.718448`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dc6ecead22bbd0255a4fbd89","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What does `dbinom(0:2, size = 2, prob = 0.3)` return?","options":["`[1] 0.49 0.91 1.00`","`[1] 0.09 0.42 0.49`","`[1] 0.49 0.21 0.09`","`[1] 0.49 0.42 0.09`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f0e030b0a39b7f1d54f5d790","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For counts, $\\mathbb{P}(X<k)$ is the `p` function at $k-1$. For $X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(4,1)$, which call returns $\\mathbb{P}(X<4)$, and why?","options":["`pnorm(3, 4, 1)`, since $\\mathbb{P}(X<k)$ is the `p` value at $k-1$.","`pnorm(4, 4, 1)`, since $\\mathbb{P}(X=4)=0$ for a continuous $X$.","`pnorm(4, 4, 1) - dnorm(4, 4, 1)`, removing the probability at 4.","`pnorm(3.999, 4, 1)`, since the largest value below 4 is needed."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"385ae4fed4ab2fbeb050ced1","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"An analyst wants $\\mathbb{P}(X>110)$ for a normal $X$ with mean 100 and variance 225, and runs `1 - pnorm(110, 100, 225)`. What does R return?","options":["`[1] 0.2524925`","`[1] 0.5177249`","`[1] 0.4822751`","`[1] 0.7475075`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dee26e22d2f6c78c65f08770","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Filling times $X$ are normal with standard deviation 2 and a mean $\\mu$ that can be set. Which expression gives the $\\mu$ for which $\\mathbb{P}(X\\le10)=0.95$?","options":["`10 - 2 * qnorm(0.95)`","`10 - 4 * qnorm(0.95)`","`10 + 2 * qnorm(0.95)`","`10 - 2 * pnorm(0.95)`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"42a2d31d4923398649694139","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_distribution_functions_rdpq_1fee043b_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For a binomial count with 8 trials and success probability 0.5, what does `pbinom(qbinom(0.3, 8, 0.5), 8, 0.5)` return?","options":["`[1] 0.3`","`[1] 3`","`[1] 0.1445313`","`[1] 0.3632813`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"da7629468517cd89fda56d29","time_limit":40}]}}